Generated solution parameter identities
======================================

Run the registered regressions from the build root:

  timeout 180 ./maxima-local --no-init --lisp=sbcl \
    --batch-string='run_testsuite(tests=[rtest_parallel]);'

Require the complete "No unexpected errors" summary and reject any
"Maxima encountered a Lisp error", even if the exit status is zero.
Run the full core and share suites on each configured Lisp as well.

MAKE-PARAM shares %RNUM across independent computations. An unprotected
INCF can lose updates, making INTERN return the same symbol for two
parameters that must be independent. A lock now protects just the increment
and captures its result. Formatting, interning and appending to the current
computation's %RNUM_LIST remain outside the lock. Explicit serial resets
still reuse the original names; no already-interned-name rejection is added.
Concurrent explicit resets or private bindings of this internal counter
are not a way to request globally distinct names.

The 25 appended test pairs load the helper, perform 23 checks and clean up.
They cover zero calls, negative and large counters, crossing the fixnum
boundary, list append semantics, explicit resets and error recovery. A seeded
generator also checks 64 varied integer ranges without consuming caller RNG.
Eight native workers make 80,000 calls using fresh per-call parameter lists;
identities, numeric range, exact final count and distinct physical workers
are checked. Additional runs cross the fixnum boundary and use bignums.
All threads are joined once, and startup waits have a five-second deadline.

The mathematical checks exercise a single SOLVE computation at serial,
caller, forced worker and fallback execution sites. Public and nested
parallel checks independently construct solution families x = n - r, y = r,
verify x + y = n exactly, and require distinct free parameters. Concurrent
SOLVE itself has a separate pre-existing concurrency failure (#58); this change
does not certify the entire solver for concurrent use. No existing
mathematical expected answer or known-failure entry is changed.

Native checks run on threaded SBCL, CCL and ECL. ECL 21.2.1's public
scheduler deliberately remains serial. On a Lisp without supported native
threads, the native entry checks a bounded serial sequence and forced
worker checks use the serial fallback. Run fixtures in an otherwise idle
test process: the shared counter and scheduler statistics are restored,
and each calculation gets a private parameter list.
